Subject: [PATCH 0016] cocoa: opt out of App Nap and size the window in points

# melco-nav-emu core patch.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
# Copyright (c) 2026 melco-nav-emu contributors
# Modifies GPL-licensed QEMU sources; applied by scripts/qemu.sh.

Both boards boot and run their firmware on the host's real clock: the CU-P2006
GPS replay and CUP28's MOST peer are driven from QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL against
wall time, and bootcheck stops on virtual time.  App Nap coalesces the timers
of a process whose window is hidden, fully occluded or on another Space, and
the guest then sees virtual time stretch against the host: a first power-on
measured in seconds of guest time becomes unpredictable minutes.  The activity
is taken for the life of the process, because the board models have no idle
state to declare: the token that beginActivityWithOptions: returns is retained
once into a file-scope static and never released.  Nothing here builds with
-fobjc-arc, so without that retain the returned object would be autoreleased
into the pool cocoa_display_init() drains, and App Nap ends the activity when
its token is deallocated.

The window sizing is a second, visual defect on a Retina host: divide by
backingScaleFactor and a 400x234 panel is laid out in device pixels, so it
occupies a quarter of its intended area.  One guest pixel per point is what
QEMU 9.1 did and what these panels are drawn for; zoom-to-fit, the default for
both boards, scales it from there.

This does not change any device model, so it cannot alter what the guest sees.

--- a/ui/cocoa.m
+++ b/ui/cocoa.m
@@ -500,10 +500,9 @@
     [[self window] setContentAspectRatio:NSMakeSize(screen.width, screen.height)];
 
     if (!([[self window] styleMask] & NSWindowStyleMaskResizable)) {
-        CGFloat width = screen.width / [[self window] backingScaleFactor];
-        CGFloat height = screen.height / [[self window] backingScaleFactor];
-
-        [[self window] setContentSize:NSMakeSize(width, height)];
+        /* One guest pixel per point, as QEMU 9.1 sized it: one per device
+         * pixel quarters a 400x234 panel on a Retina display. */
+        [[self window] setContentSize:NSMakeSize(screen.width, screen.height)];
         [[self window] center];
     } else if ([[self window] styleMask] & NSWindowStyleMaskFullScreen) {
         [[self window] setContentSize:[self fixAspectRatio:[self screenSafeAreaSize]]];
@@ -1961,6 +1960,14 @@
     });
 }
 
+/*
+ * The App Nap activity taken in cocoa_display_init(). App Nap ends the activity
+ * when its token is deallocated; there is no ARC here, so the token comes back
+ * autoreleased into the pool cocoa_display_init() drains and has to be retained
+ * and never released to keep the activity alive for the life of the process.
+ */
+static id appNapActivity;
+
 static void cocoa_display_init(DisplayState *ds, DisplayOptions *opts)
 {
     NSAutoreleasePool * pool = [[NSAutoreleasePool alloc] init];
@@ -1974,6 +1981,18 @@
 
     [QemuApplication sharedApplication];
 
+    /*
+     * Opt out of App Nap. Both machines run their firmware against the host
+     * clock and a boot is measured in guest time, so a hidden or occluded
+     * window must not have its timers coalesced. The token is retained into
+     * appNapActivity, which nothing releases: see its declaration.
+     */
+    appNapActivity =
+        [[[NSProcessInfo processInfo]
+           beginActivityWithOptions:NSActivityUserInitiated |
+                                    NSActivityLatencyCritical
+                             reason:@"emulating hardware in real time"] retain];
+
     // Create an Application controller
     QemuCocoaAppController *controller = [[QemuCocoaAppController alloc] init];
     [NSApp setDelegate:controller];
